// A visibility modifier on an EXTENSION HEAD is a declaration-site error
// (ruled Aug 20). An extension is not a nameable entity — nothing imports it,
// nothing calls it, there is no name for a modifier to govern — so what an
// importer reaches is its MEMBERS, and design 80 already gives each of those
// its own modifier.
//
// The marker used to parse and be stored, with exactly one consumer: the docs
// emitter's signature string. It was not a member default (an unmarked member
// inside a `public extension` was PRIVATE), not a clamp (a `public` member
// inside a `public(package)` one was world-callable), and not a scoping input
// (design 142 scopes extensions by IMPORT). Decoration that read like a rule,
// on ~105 corpus heads — which is also why adopting Swift's default-setter
// meaning was rejected: it would have flipped every unmarked member inside one
// from private to public in silence.
//
// The refusal is anchored at the modifier, the token to delete, and every
// spelling reaches it: `public`, `public(package)`, `public(parent)`, and the
// `@synthesize`-attributed head (`attr_synthesize_parses.saw` covers the legal
// attributed form).
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: an extension cannot carry a visibility modifier

public struct Point { public x: Int }

public extension Point {
    public func get(&self) -> Int { self.x }
}

func main() { print(Point(x: 1).get()) }
